
# Project 02 - Container Resource Management

Campus Equipment Booking System

## Project Overview

This project explores container resource management using Docker Compose, Apache HTTPD and PostgreSQL within a simulated campus equipment booking environment.

The project focuses on CPU and memory restrictions, resource monitoring, service availability, network segmentation, container hardening, persistent storage and database disaster recovery.

## Technical Objectives

- Deploy Apache HTTPD and PostgreSQL containers.
- Configure individual CPU and memory restrictions.
- Monitor container resource utilisation.
- Implement automatic restart policies.
- Implement frontend and backend network segmentation.
- Apply least-privilege security controls.
- Maintain persistent database storage.
- Perform database backup and restoration.

## Service Configuration

| Parameter | Web Service | Database Service |
|---|---|---|
| Service | booking | database |
| Container | booking-web | booking-db |
| Image | httpd:2.4 | postgres:16 |
| Published Port | 8112:80 | Disabled |
| CPU Limit | 0.7 | 0.6 |
| Memory Limit | 352 MiB | 576 MiB |
| Restart Policy | unless-stopped | always |

## Network Architecture

| Network | Driver | Connected Services |
|---|---|---|
| booking-front | bridge | Web |
| booking-back | bridge | Web and Database |

The database is restricted to the backend network without a published host port.

## Docker Compose Configuration

```yaml
services:
  booking:
    image: httpd:2.4
    container_name: booking-web
    ports:
      - "8112:80"
    cpus: 0.7
    mem_limit: 352m
    restart: unless-stopped
    networks:
      - booking-front
      - booking-back
    privileged: false
    cap_drop:
      - ALL
    security_opt:
      - no-new-privileges:true

  database:
    image: postgres:16
    container_name: booking-db
    environment:
      POSTGRES_DB: bookingdb
      POSTGRES_USER: bookinguser
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:?Set password}
    cpus: 0.6
    mem_limit: 576m
    restart: always
    networks:
      - booking-back
    volumes:
      - booking-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

networks:
  booking-front:
    name: booking-front
    driver: bridge

  booking-back:
    name: booking-back
    driver: bridge

volumes:
  booking-data:
    name: booking-data
```

## Security Controls

| Control | Purpose |
|---|---|
| cpus | Restrict container CPU consumption |
| mem_limit | Restrict container memory consumption |
| Restart policies | Support automatic service recovery |
| privileged: false | Avoid privileged execution |
| cap_drop: ALL | Remove Linux capabilities |
| no-new-privileges | Prevent additional privilege acquisition |
| Network segmentation | Restrict unnecessary communication |
| Database port isolation | Reduce direct database exposure |

## Deployment and Verification

```powershell
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps
docker stats --no-stream
docker inspect booking-web --format '{{.HostConfig.NanoCpus}}'
docker inspect booking-web --format '{{.HostConfig.Memory}}'
docker inspect booking-db --format '{{.HostConfig.NanoCpus}}'
docker inspect booking-db --format '{{.HostConfig.Memory}}'
docker network inspect booking-front
docker network inspect booking-back
docker logs booking-web
docker exec booking-db pg_isready -U bookinguser -d bookingdb
```

Web service testing is performed using localhost on port 8112.

## Resource Verification

| Container | Expected NanoCPUs | Expected Memory in Bytes |
|---|---|---|
| booking-web | 700000000 | 369098752 |
| booking-db | 600000000 | 603979776 |

Resource verification focuses on confirming that individual containers operate within their configured CPU and memory restrictions.

## Persistent Storage and Recovery

| Parameter | Configuration |
|---|---|
| Database | bookingdb |
| Test Table | reservations |
| Original Volume | booking-data |
| Backup Directory | C:\BookingBackup |
| Backup Archive | booking-data.tar.gz |
| Restored Volume | booking-restored |

The recovery procedure involves stopping PostgreSQL, creating a physical volume backup, restoring the archive into a separate volume and verifying the original database records.

The final Compose configuration uses booking-restored as an external volume after restoration.

